<h2>Why I do this (and why I don't pay to amplify)</h2>

<p>Social media exists in this agency for one reason: prospective clients sometimes look at my profile before they book a discovery call. A barren or inactive profile reads as "this person stopped working last year." A profile that's been quietly active for two years &mdash; thoughtful posts at a consistent rhythm, no chasing trends, no growth-hacky engagement bait &mdash; reads as "this person is doing real work and thinking about it in public." That is the entire goal.</p>

<p>I never pay to promote a post. Boosted posts and platform ads are a separate operation with their own economics, their own measurement burden, and their own way of distorting what gets posted (you start writing for the algorithm instead of for the reader). The whole agency runs on systems I own end-to-end &mdash; content, relationships, outreach &mdash; and paid social would be the one part where the platform owns the relationship and I rent it. That trade is not worth making at this scale.</p>

<p>The 30-minute daily cap is load-bearing. Without it, social media is a black hole that consumes thinking time and contributes nothing to client work. With it, social media is a finite, scheduled habit that contributes a small but real top-of-funnel signal.</p>

<h2>Platforms I commit to</h2>

<p><strong>Primary: LinkedIn.</strong> The single platform where the buyers I'm writing for &mdash; small-business owners, agency principals, business coaches, trade-association leaders &mdash; actually spend professional time. Posts get organic reach, comments are usually substantive, and the platform doesn't require chasing a content format.</p>

<p><strong>Secondary: one of {X, Bluesky, Mastodon}.</strong> I rotate this annually based on where my industry conversation is happening. The criterion is "where do other independent web operators talk shop"; that has migrated platforms several times in recent years. The secondary is a one-post-per-week commitment, not a real audience-building exercise.</p>

<p><strong>Read-only on the rest.</strong> Instagram, TikTok, YouTube Shorts, Facebook, Threads. I have accounts (or claimed handles) on each, with a profile photo and a one-line bio pointing back to the agency site, but I do not post. The accounts exist so a curious prospect can verify the brand identity, not as an audience-building channel.</p>

<p>If a platform I'm not active on starts driving meaningful inbound (a discovery-call form submission attributes itself to "found you on YouTube"), I revisit. So far, no.</p>

<h2>Posting cadence by platform</h2>

<p><strong>LinkedIn:</strong> 3 posts per week. Tuesday morning, Thursday morning, one floater between Friday and Sunday. Morning meaning 8-9am Mountain Time, which catches the East Coast lunch hour and the West Coast pre-work scroll.</p>

<p><strong>Secondary platform:</strong> 1-2 posts per week. No fixed days. Repurposed from a LinkedIn post that landed well, often with a tighter or more conversational tone for the format.</p>

<p><strong>Both platforms combined:</strong> 4-5 posts per week, all batched on Sunday and scheduled out via a Buffer-class tool. None of these are written and posted live. Live posting compounds into the daily-cap problem.</p>

<h2>Post types</h2>

<p>Five categories, in roughly the rotation I aim for over a month:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Insight (40% of posts).</strong> A small observation about how I run the agency, a technical decision and the reason behind it, or a pattern I've noticed across client engagements. Two paragraphs, no hashtags, no call to action.</li>
  <li><strong>Build-in-public (20%).</strong> A specific thing I shipped this week, what was harder than expected, what I'd do differently. Concrete, with numbers where possible. Not "we just shipped X!"&mdash;more like "shipped X, here's the part that took longest and why."</li>
  <li><strong>Client win, with permission (15%).</strong> Outcome-focused, named client only if they explicitly approved the post wording. Default is anonymized: "a roofer in the Front Range" instead of "Redcap Roofing." Always credits the client first; the agency contribution is one sentence at the end.</li>
  <li><strong>Repurposed blog excerpt (15%).</strong> Pulled from a recent long-form post. Standalone read &mdash; should make sense without clicking through. Link to the full post is in the first comment, not the post body (LinkedIn deprioritizes link-out posts).</li>
  <li><strong>Evergreen tip (10%).</strong> A small operational practice useful to someone running a service business or building their own site. Decoupled from the agency's offerings.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Repurposing rhythm</h2>

<p>One blog post fans into roughly six social posts spread over fourteen days:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Day 0 (post publishes):</strong> LinkedIn announcement of the post itself, with the headline and one teaser paragraph. Link in the first comment.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 2:</strong> The post's strongest standalone passage as its own LinkedIn insight post. No mention of the source article; works on its own.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 4:</strong> Secondary platform repost of the Day 2 passage, conversationally rewritten for the format.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 7:</strong> A specific point or example from the post, framed as a build-in-public observation. LinkedIn.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> A counterpoint or "what most people get wrong about this" framing of one section's argument. LinkedIn.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 14:</strong> The post's closing takeaway as an evergreen tip. Either platform.</li>
</ol>

<p>This makes one well-researched 1,200-word blog post the source material for two-plus weeks of social presence. It is the same material the newsletter and the cold-outreach footer also draw from. One piece of writing, four channels.</p>

<h2>Drafting workflow</h2>

<p><strong>Sunday afternoon, 60-90 minutes.</strong> I open the Buffer-class scheduling tool, the recent blog post, and last week's posting analytics. I draft 4-5 LinkedIn posts and 1-2 secondary-platform posts for the upcoming week. Each is scheduled with a specific publish time. The week's queue is fully loaded before Sunday evening.</p>

<p>The Sunday batch protects the daily 30-minute cap from inflating into a daily 90-minute trap. Without batching, every day starts with "what do I post today" and ends with that question still unresolved. With batching, the daily 30 minutes is reserved purely for engagement (replying to comments, scrolling enough to know what conversations are happening, checking for DMs from real prospects).</p>

<p>The drafts are not perfect. The 80%-is-shipped principle applies here as it does to client work. A post that took 8 minutes to write and got 3 substantive replies is more valuable than a post that took 40 minutes and got the same result.</p>

<h2>Comment + DM policy</h2>

<p><strong>Comments on my own posts:</strong> reply to every substantive comment within 24 hours. "Thanks!" replies are fine in the first 24 hours; after that the post has aged out of the feed and replying serves no one. Replies in the first hour boost the post's distribution; LinkedIn's algorithm rewards early engagement.</p>

<p><strong>Comments on others' posts:</strong> only when I have something genuinely additive to say. Never the "great post!" engagement-hack reply &mdash; it is read as spam by the people whose attention I'm trying to earn. About 10-15 outbound substantive comments per week, mostly on posts from other independent web operators or small-business operators in trades I work with.</p>

<p><strong>DMs:</strong> respond within one business day. DMs from real prospects (someone considering a project) get routed to the discovery-call flow &mdash; I send the same booking link from the contact page rather than running the entire conversation in DMs. DMs from spammers, recruiters, and platform-services pitches get a brief polite no, not silence; silence trains them to keep trying.</p>

<p><strong>Things I never do:</strong> automated follow / unfollow, bulk DM outreach, engagement-pod participation, pay-to-play comment schemes, "thoughts?" engagement-bait posts, polls designed to manipulate the algorithm. Each of these works on the platform's terms but undermines the credibility I'm there to build.</p>

<h2>Account hygiene</h2>

<p><strong>Quarterly:</strong> review the profile end-to-end. Profile photo current? Cover image current? Headline reflects what the agency actually does? About section accurate? Featured posts pinned to the most representative recent work? Outdated experience entries removed?</p>

<p><strong>Monthly:</strong> refresh the pinned post. The pinned post is the most-seen artifact on the profile, more than any individual feed post. It should rotate to whatever the most-current piece of work is &mdash; a recent case study, a long-form blog post, a notable engagement.</p>

<p><strong>Annually:</strong> export connections list, audit who I'm following, prune dormant accounts and serial spammers. The "follow" graph degrades over time as people change roles, get acquired, or go quiet; a 30-minute prune restores the feed's signal-to-noise.</p>

<h2>What to do when a post gets traction (and what not to do)</h2>

<p>Occasionally a post catches the algorithm and gets meaningfully more reach than the average. The instinct is to do something about it &mdash; double down on that topic, post a follow-up the next day, push for more reach.</p>

<p>What I actually do:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Reply to every substantive comment in the first six hours. The early-engagement window is when the algorithm decides whether to push the post further.</li>
  <li>Note the post's topic and angle in a small "what landed" log. Patterns over a quarter are useful; reactive same-day decisions usually aren't.</li>
  <li>Resist the urge to repost the same content 24 hours later. The fraction of audience overlap is high; the same content twice within 48 hours reads as spam to anyone who saw it the first time.</li>
  <li>Stick to the planned schedule. The next post is whatever was queued for the next slot.</li>
</ol>

<p>The wrong move is treating one well-performing post as a strategy. The post that performs is usually a function of timing, an arbitrary algorithm boost, and the prior dozen posts that built the audience that engaged with this one. Reverse-engineering "the formula" from a single hit produces a few weeks of imitation posts and then a slow decline back to baseline.</p>

<h2>Kill rules</h2>

<p>A platform earns its 30-minute weekly slice or it gets cut. The thresholds:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>If LinkedIn reach has dropped 50%+ over a rolling 90-day window with no policy or strategy change on my end:</strong> review whether the platform is materially changing. Don't kill yet; LinkedIn's algorithm shifts every 6-12 months, but signals get noisy.</li>
  <li><strong>If a secondary platform produces zero substantive engagement (no real comments, no real DMs from prospects) for 90 days:</strong> rotate it out and pick a different secondary, or drop the secondary entirely for a quarter to reclaim the time.</li>
  <li><strong>If maintaining a platform consistently exceeds the 30-minute daily cap:</strong> the cap is inviolable; the platform's commitment level drops first.</li>
</ul>

<p>Kill criteria run the other direction too. If a platform I'm read-only on starts producing inbound &mdash; a prospect mentions finding me there &mdash; I revisit whether to start posting. So far that has not happened. The signal would be unmistakable when it does.</p>

